# Line endings
#
# Git stores text with LF and checks it out with the platform's native ending: CRLF in
# the working tree on Windows, LF everywhere else. Without this file that decision comes
# from core.autocrlf, which is a per-machine setting -- so the same commit produced a
# different diff for whoever cloned it, which is exactly the churn this stops.
#
# These attributes win over core.autocrlf, so the behaviour is the same for everyone
# regardless of how their Git is configured.

* text=auto

# The text we actually ship. Redundant under `text=auto` above, but spelled out: a
# migration's line endings are never cosmetic, and a .sql file is the one file type here
# whose bytes another tool reads directly.
#
# `linguist-language=SQL` overrides GitHub's language stats. Four Linguist languages claim
# `.sql` -- SQL, TSQL, PLpgSQL and PLSQL -- so the extension alone is ambiguous and Linguist
# falls back to scoring file contents, which put migrations/019_customer_identity.sql in
# PLpgSQL on the strength of its DECLARE/FOR/BEGIN-END blocks. That is a Postgres procedure
# body, not what this file is.
#
# This overrides detection, and detection owns the tm_scope, so highlighting moves from TSQL
# (source.tsql) to SQL (source.sql). Ordinary DDL and DML still highlight; GO, #temp tables
# and [bracketed] identifiers are the constructs that stop looking right. Swap the value to
# TSQL to get those back at the cost of the bar reading "TSQL" instead of "SQL".
#
# Nothing here touches line endings: `text` still decides that, as above.
*.py      text
*.sql     text
*.md      text
*.txt     text
*.ini     text
*.example text

# Never translate these. A CR inside a CSV cell or a header byte in an image is data
# corruption, not a formatting preference, and git's binary guess is not good enough to
# be trusted on a generated export.
*.csv   -text
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.db    binary
*.bak   binary
